{
  "image_name": "1cef5d8cd79b1d81a6fa461d6b3a339c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two host defense pathways triggered by bacterial toxins at an epithelial surface. On the left, an exotoxin directly inhibits host protein translation. This lowers overall translation but, through upstream open reading frames, appears to favor translation of ZIP-2. ZIP-2 then partners with CEBP-2 in the nucleus to activate innate immune effectors, producing an antipathogen response.\n\nThe center and right side focus on mitochondria and ATFS-1. Under healthy conditions, ATFS-1 is imported into mitochondria via its targeting sequence and is degraded there, so the mitochondrial unfolded protein response remains off. In contrast, a bacterial toxin induces mitochondrial stress and blocks protein import into mitochondria. As a result, ATFS-1 accumulates in the cytosol instead of entering the organelle. Because ATFS-1 also carries a nuclear localization signal, it relocates to the nucleus, where it activates the mitochondrial unfolded protein response. This transcriptional program is linked to detoxification and pathogen defense.\n\nOverall, the image shows that pathogens can trigger immunity through two distinct but complementary routes: one based on translational inhibition and one based on mitochondrial dysfunction and ATFS-1 signaling.",
